<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oshun</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ọṣun, Ochún, Oxúm</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oshun is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orisha">orisha</a> of love, fertility, water, divination, wealth and beauty in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> religion, and the patron of the Osun River in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, which bears her name; she is one of the most popular and venerated of the orishas. According to traditional beliefs she was once a queen of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shango">Shango</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a> and was deified after her death. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifa">Ifá</a> literary corpus she is the only female primordial spirit sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olorun">Olodumare</a> to help create the world. She is honoured each August at the two-week <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osun-osogbo">Osun-Osogbo</a> Festival in the sacred grove at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osogbo">Osogbo</a>, and her worship continues in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oshun.jpg" alt="Oshun" width="330" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wikipedia Loves Art participant &quot; Department_of_Trife · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WLA_haa_Kneeling_Female_Figure_Devotee_of_Oshun_Yoruba_people_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The sacred grove of the river goddess <b>Oshun</b> at Osogbo in Nigeria, one of the last stretches of primary high forest in southern Nigeria, was restored with the help of the Austrian artist Susanne Wenger, who became a priestess of the orisha, and is now a World Heritage…</blockquote><a href="/religion/yoruba-religion">Yoruba religion · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
